# ============================================================
# HW2 – Redis – Part A
# TASK: Back-in-stock waitlist ("notify me when the size is back in stock")
#       FIFO queue with duplicate prevention
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Data type and key schema:
#
#   waitlist:{sku}             Sorted Set   member = userId, score = time of joining
#       One waitlist per sold-out variant. SKU = product + size + colour,
#       e.g. p13-M-white (the same SKU format as in the cart task).
#       The score is the Unix time (seconds) when the customer clicked
#       "Notify me", so the order of the set is the order of arrival.
#
# What the task does:
#   - a customer joins the waitlist of a sold-out size (ZADD NX);
#   - a customer who clicks "Notify me" again is NOT added a second time and
#     keeps the original place in the queue (NX = only add new members);
#   - the customer sees his position in the queue (ZRANK) and the shop sees
#     the whole queue in the order of arrival (ZRANGE);
#   - a customer can leave the waitlist (ZREM);
#   - when N pieces of the size come back in stock, the first N customers
#     are taken from the queue in FIFO order and notified (ZPOPMIN key N);
#   - requests older than 30 days are removed by time (ZREMRANGEBYSCORE).
#
# Result: the queue in the order of arrival, 0 for a duplicate request (the
# queue does not change), the position of a customer, the customers that
# are notified first when the size is back in stock, and the queue after
# a cancellation, a notification and a cleanup.
#
# Why Redis: a waitlist is short-lived state that changes on every click,
# so it is kept in memory. A sorted set gives both properties of the task in
# ONE atomic command: ZADD NX ignores a member that already exists (no
# duplicates, no check-then-insert race when the button is clicked twice) and
# the score keeps the FIFO order. ZPOPMIN removes and returns the first N
# customers atomically, so two workers processing a restock can never notify
# the same customer twice. A plain List + Set would need an "if" in the
# application (or a Lua script) to prevent duplicates, which a sorted set
# does natively. Requires Redis 6.2+ (ZMSCORE).
#
# Product ids and sold-out variants (stock 0) are taken from HW1 (MongoDB):
#   p13-M-white  Graphic Tee, M, white
#   p09-42-black Urban Sneaker, 42, black
#   p15-42-brown Chelsea Boot, 42, brown
# User ids u1–u12 are the same customers as in the cart task.
#
# Run (redis-cli does not accept comment lines):
#   grep -v '^#' waitlist.redis | grep -v '^$' | redis-cli
# ============================================================

# Clean start
DEL waitlist:p13-M-white waitlist:p09-42-black waitlist:p15-42-brown


# ---------- Test data: 14 waitlist entries of 12 customers ----------
# Score = Unix time of joining. 1795000000 = 2026-11-18 11:06:40 UTC.
# u1 and u2 wait for two different sizes – every waitlist is independent.

# Graphic Tee, M, white – 7 customers
# order of arrival: u3 (+0 min), u7 (+10), u1 (+20), u9 (+50), u5 (+2 h), u2 (+3 h), u11 (+4 h)
# Expected result: 7
ZADD waitlist:p13-M-white NX 1795000000 u3 1795000600 u7 1795001200 u1 1795003000 u9 1795007200 u5 1795010800 u2 1795014400 u11

# Urban Sneaker, 42, black – 4 customers
# Expected result: 4
ZADD waitlist:p09-42-black NX 1795002000 u4 1795004000 u1 1795006000 u8 1795009000 u10

# Chelsea Boot, 42, brown – 3 customers, u12 joined already on 2026-10-14
# Expected result: 3
ZADD waitlist:p15-42-brown NX 1792000000 u12 1795005000 u6 1795008000 u2


# ---------- 1. Length of every waitlist ----------
# Expected result: 7, 4, 3
ZCARD waitlist:p13-M-white
ZCARD waitlist:p09-42-black
ZCARD waitlist:p15-42-brown


# ---------- 2. The whole queue in the order of arrival ----------
# Expected result: u3, u7, u1, u9, u5, u2, u11 (with their join times)
ZRANGE waitlist:p13-M-white 0 -1 WITHSCORES


# ---------- 3. Duplicate request – the customer clicks "Notify me" again ----------
# u1 is already waiting (joined at 1795001200) and clicks again later.
# NX: the existing member is not changed.
# Expected result: 0 (nothing added)
ZADD waitlist:p13-M-white NX 1795020000 u1
# The original join time is kept, so u1 does not lose the place in the queue.
# (A plain ZADD without NX would overwrite the score and move u1 to the end.)
# Expected result: 1795001200
ZSCORE waitlist:p13-M-white u1
# The queue still has 7 customers – u1 is not in it twice.
# Expected result: 7
ZCARD waitlist:p13-M-white
# Position of u1 (0-based) – the page shows "You are number 3 in the queue".
# Expected result: 2
ZRANK waitlist:p13-M-white u1


# ---------- 4. A new customer joins ----------
# Expected result: 1 (one new customer added)
ZADD waitlist:p13-M-white NX 1795018000 u6
# The new customer is at the end of the queue.
# Expected result: 7 (8th in the queue) and 8 customers in total
ZRANK waitlist:p13-M-white u6
ZCARD waitlist:p13-M-white


# ---------- 5. Is the customer on this waitlist? ----------
# u1 is waiting for Graphic Tee M white, u4 is not (u4 waits for the sneaker).
# Expected result: 1795001200 (u1 is waiting), nil (u4 is not)
ZMSCORE waitlist:p13-M-white u1 u4


# ---------- 6. A customer leaves the waitlist ----------
# u9 bought another size and cancels the request.
# Expected result: 1 (removed)
ZREM waitlist:p13-M-white u9
# Customers behind u9 move up: u5 was number 5, now number 4.
# Expected result: 3
ZRANK waitlist:p13-M-white u5


# ---------- 7. The size is back in stock – notify in FIFO order ----------
# 3 pieces of Graphic Tee M white arrived. The first 3 customers are taken
# from the queue and notified; the operation is atomic.
# Expected result: u3, u7, u1 (with their join times) – exactly the first three
ZPOPMIN waitlist:p13-M-white 3
# The rest of the queue keeps waiting in the same order.
# Expected result: u5, u2, u11, u6
ZRANGE waitlist:p13-M-white 0 -1
# u1 is still waiting for the sneaker – the waitlists are independent.
# Expected result: 1795004000
ZSCORE waitlist:p09-42-black u1


# ---------- 8. A notified customer may join again ----------
# u3 was notified (no longer in the queue), so a new request is accepted,
# but u3 goes to the end of the queue like any new customer.
# Expected result: 1 (added), then 4 (5th in the queue)
ZADD waitlist:p13-M-white NX 1795030000 u3
ZRANK waitlist:p13-M-white u3


# ---------- 9. Cleanup of old requests ----------
# Now = 1795400000 (2026-11-23). Requests older than 30 days
# (joined before 1795400000 - 2592000 = 1792808000) are removed by time.
# Expected result: 1 (u12 – joined on 2026-10-14)
ZREMRANGEBYSCORE waitlist:p15-42-brown -inf 1792808000
# Expected result: u6, u2
ZRANGE waitlist:p15-42-brown 0 -1


# ---------- 10. More pieces arrived than customers are waiting ----------
# 10 pieces of Urban Sneaker 42 black arrived, only 4 customers are waiting.
# Expected result: u4, u1, u8, u10 – all of them in the order of arrival
ZPOPMIN waitlist:p09-42-black 10
# Redis deletes an empty sorted set automatically – no cleanup needed.
# Expected result: 0 (the waitlist no longer exists)
EXISTS waitlist:p09-42-black
